00:00:53.737 --> 00:00:55.506
which we gave out
on the first day,

00:00:55.606 --> 00:00:56.974
please get in touch with Laura.

00:00:57.074 --> 00:00:58.042
And she'll give you one.

00:00:58.142 --> 00:00:59.343
We have extras here.

00:00:59.443 --> 00:01:03.747
You can stop by our office
later, get one please today.

00:01:03.847 --> 00:01:08.952
All right, now, last
lecture, we left off with--

00:01:09.053 --> 00:01:14.658
remember, those guys like Boyle
and Priestley and Lavoisier,

00:01:14.758 --> 00:01:16.460
conservation of mass, right?

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:20.230
And what these
guys were doing is

00:01:20.330 --> 00:01:24.268
they were smashing and
burning and pushing on things

00:01:24.368 --> 00:01:28.038
and trying to figure
out what it was

00:01:28.138 --> 00:01:32.776
that was that sort
of fundamental object

00:01:32.876 --> 00:01:35.412
that you couldn't
break apart anymore.

00:01:35.512 --> 00:01:38.247
And remember, it was
Democritus that said

00:01:38.348 --> 00:01:40.283
there is such a thing, right?

00:01:40.384 --> 00:01:44.922
He said there was a such a
thing called atoms, "atomos".

00:01:45.021 --> 00:01:46.557
And so we come to
Dalton, who said,

00:01:46.657 --> 00:01:48.158
look, these things
we're trying, we're

00:01:48.258 --> 00:01:50.727
calling them elements right now.

00:01:50.828 --> 00:01:54.832
Let's, in honor of that
indivisibility idea,

00:01:54.932 --> 00:01:57.601
let's call them atoms.

00:01:57.701 --> 00:02:01.472
And Dalton did a lot of
really important experiments.

00:02:01.572 --> 00:02:05.275
And he was also trying to
smash and break and measure

00:02:05.375 --> 00:02:08.544
and figure out what
are these indivisible--

00:02:08.645 --> 00:02:12.816
and what are these things that
you can't break apart any more?

00:02:12.916 --> 00:02:14.051
What are these atoms?

00:02:14.151 --> 00:02:16.153
How many of them are there?

00:02:16.253 --> 00:02:17.888
What do they do?

00:02:17.988 --> 00:02:22.593
And he came up with a
pretty important observation

00:02:22.693 --> 00:02:28.198
which was that he called it the
law of multiple proportions.

00:02:28.298 --> 00:02:35.038
And in this, so what
Dalton did is he said,

00:02:35.139 --> 00:02:39.109
look, I'm going to take
something and react it

00:02:39.209 --> 00:02:40.811
with something else.

00:02:40.911 --> 00:02:43.280
And it's going to react fully.

00:02:43.380 --> 00:02:45.616
OK, so there's no
limiting reagent.

00:02:45.716 --> 00:02:46.884
There's no excess.

00:02:46.984 --> 00:02:49.286
It just everything reacted
here and everything

00:02:49.386 --> 00:02:52.189
and want to measure
how much stuff reacts.

00:02:52.289 --> 00:02:54.291
And then how much I get.

00:02:54.391 --> 00:02:57.194
And so if you take
like an example

00:02:57.293 --> 00:03:02.065
of this law of
multiple proportions,

00:03:02.166 --> 00:03:04.601
if you take an example
of how that came about,

00:03:04.701 --> 00:03:08.605
what he observed is that if
you had sort of, for example,

00:03:08.705 --> 00:03:12.409
maybe you had sort of case one.

00:03:12.509 --> 00:03:14.978
And you react some amount.

00:03:15.078 --> 00:03:17.079
So he's going to
react some amount.

00:03:17.181 --> 00:03:19.583
So let's say he reacted--

00:03:19.683 --> 00:03:21.985
OK, I don't know, some amount.

00:03:22.085 --> 00:03:23.353
Let's write it.

00:03:23.453 --> 00:03:28.492
Yeah, some amount of
something, maybe, carbon.

00:03:28.591 --> 00:03:33.597
And some amount
of something else.

00:03:33.697 --> 00:03:35.432
How about oxygen?

00:03:35.531 --> 00:03:38.569
And he would get
some amount, right?

00:03:38.668 --> 00:03:41.672
OK, we know he's doing
all these measurements.

00:03:41.772 --> 00:03:43.540
I'm trying to get you
back in that mood.

00:03:43.640 --> 00:03:44.474
How much do I have?

00:03:44.575 --> 00:03:45.942
I'm going to weigh
it, measure it.

00:03:46.043 --> 00:03:47.477
That's what he's
doing over and over.

00:03:47.578 --> 00:03:51.281
But he had some
amount of something.

00:03:51.381 --> 00:03:53.350
Aha.

00:03:53.450 --> 00:03:56.653
Now, he would do this
over and over again.

00:03:56.753 --> 00:03:59.623
OK, I'm just taking these
two elements as an example.

00:03:59.723 --> 00:04:02.492
He did this for many,
many elements, right?

00:04:02.593 --> 00:04:04.428
So some amount of
carbon with salmonella.

00:04:04.528 --> 00:04:08.432
And in case one, OK,
everything reacts.

00:04:08.532 --> 00:04:16.072
And I get this carbon to
oxygen mass ratio of let's say

00:04:16.173 --> 00:04:19.875
1 to 1.33.

00:04:19.976 --> 00:04:20.543
I measure it.

00:04:23.380 --> 00:04:26.350
That's how much of
the sum amount, right?

00:04:26.450 --> 00:04:31.555
1 to 1.33 reacts fully.

00:04:31.655 --> 00:04:34.591
Remember, no excess, because
they add a little more,

00:04:34.691 --> 00:04:35.359
and it's excess.

00:04:35.459 --> 00:04:38.462
No, this reacts fully.

00:04:38.562 --> 00:04:41.331
And then you do
it, again, case 2.

00:04:41.431 --> 00:04:47.771
And he'd find that this
same ratio was 1 to 2.66.

00:04:47.871 --> 00:04:49.139
Ha, ha.

00:04:49.239 --> 00:04:51.642
And he noticed a pattern.

00:04:51.742 --> 00:04:53.210
And he noticed a pattern.

00:04:53.310 --> 00:04:55.012
And he called it the
law multiple portions,

00:04:55.112 --> 00:04:58.815
because what he found is
that you could keep on going.

00:04:58.915 --> 00:05:01.918
And in some cases,
not all cases.

00:05:02.019 --> 00:05:04.121
But in some cases,
like carbon and oxygen,

00:05:04.221 --> 00:05:10.627
for example, these were
common ratios, these series.

00:05:10.727 --> 00:05:15.465
2.66 divided by
1.33 is an integer.

00:05:15.565 --> 00:05:20.804
These things would happen in
integer ratios, integer amount.

00:05:20.904 --> 00:05:24.574
So I could keep going, and
maybe I'd get three or four.

00:05:24.675 --> 00:05:25.542
Right?

00:05:25.642 --> 00:05:27.311
And what that
means is that well,

00:05:27.411 --> 00:05:30.180
over here, this sum
amount of something

00:05:30.280 --> 00:05:33.817
is it said something
about the something,

00:05:33.917 --> 00:05:40.557
right, because it says maybe
these somethings are like CO

00:05:40.657 --> 00:05:45.495
and CO2, right, for example.

00:05:45.595 --> 00:05:49.533
Maybe, aha, but not definitely.

00:05:49.633 --> 00:05:51.068
And that frustrated Dalton.

00:05:51.168 --> 00:05:52.602
That frustrated him.

00:05:52.703 --> 00:05:54.571
We'll come back
to that in a sec.

00:05:54.671 --> 00:05:56.306
Right?

00:05:56.406 --> 00:05:57.541
But he did this.

00:05:57.641 --> 00:06:02.679
And he observed these
ratios, these integer ratios

00:06:02.779 --> 00:06:05.782
of what would react with what--
he's trying to figure out

00:06:05.882 --> 00:06:08.085
what is he making?

00:06:08.185 --> 00:06:09.386
All right, what is he making?

00:06:12.556 --> 00:06:16.293
Now, he also, as with
Lavoisier, remember,

00:06:16.393 --> 00:06:19.096
I showed you
Lavoisier's tableau.

00:06:19.196 --> 00:06:22.165
Well, he had his own table.

00:06:22.265 --> 00:06:23.467
And here it is.

00:06:23.567 --> 00:06:29.840
So here in 1827, he had
identified 36 elements.

00:06:29.940 --> 00:06:32.743
Now, the thing is,
though, OK, 0.1,

00:06:32.843 --> 00:06:35.645
if you're going to try to brand
something, which he clearly

00:06:35.746 --> 00:06:37.514
tried with these
symbols, you can't make

00:06:37.614 --> 00:06:40.317
them this complicated, right?

00:06:40.417 --> 00:06:43.920
I'm pretty happy that that's
not how we write our elements.

00:06:44.020 --> 00:06:45.622
You can see that this
would be very hard.

00:06:45.722 --> 00:06:47.824
But he did come up
with these symbols.

00:06:47.924 --> 00:06:50.093
And you can see it would
be very hard to keep going

00:06:50.193 --> 00:06:51.428
without getting [inaudible].

00:06:51.528 --> 00:06:53.697
So I think we're pretty lucky
that [? brazilious ?] came

00:06:53.797 --> 00:06:56.400
along and figured out how
to name these elements

00:06:56.500 --> 00:06:59.336
in a consistent way.

00:06:59.436 --> 00:07:00.737
But that wasn't
his real problem.

00:07:00.837 --> 00:07:04.174
His real problem was
what I just alluded to.

00:07:04.274 --> 00:07:08.879
See, he knew that he had
these different elements.

00:07:08.979 --> 00:07:11.648
But he didn't know what
the compositions were.

00:07:11.748 --> 00:07:13.984
He was so close.

00:07:14.084 --> 00:07:15.318
He was so close.

00:07:15.419 --> 00:07:16.653
He could see that these--

00:07:16.753 --> 00:07:19.289
ah, but is it CO and CO2?

00:07:19.389 --> 00:07:21.957
Or is it CO2 and CO4?

00:07:22.058 --> 00:07:23.727
That he didn't know.

00:07:23.827 --> 00:07:27.531
That he didn't know, because
he didn't have an accurate way

00:07:27.631 --> 00:07:31.635
to measure the mass of an
atom, not at that time.

00:07:31.735 --> 00:07:36.473
But he did have this nice
ability to see these series.

00:07:36.573 --> 00:07:37.307
OK.

00:07:37.407 --> 00:07:40.409
But in comes other measurements.

00:07:40.510 --> 00:07:47.117
And you had Gay-Lussac who
was looking at volumes, right?

00:07:47.217 --> 00:07:48.752
And so Gay-Lussac
was saying, well,

00:07:48.852 --> 00:07:54.791
look, if I take a certain amount
of volume of a gas, one gas,

00:07:54.891 --> 00:07:59.062
and I reacted again
completely, no access, OK?

00:07:59.162 --> 00:08:01.898
And I reacted with another gas.

00:08:01.998 --> 00:08:05.035
And then I see I get
something else over here.

00:08:05.135 --> 00:08:06.436
I can measure the volumes.

00:08:06.536 --> 00:08:08.805
And I do this for
the cases where

00:08:08.905 --> 00:08:12.909
there's nothing left
over, nothing limited it.

00:08:13.009 --> 00:08:17.614
And so he would find, for
example, what's shown up there.

00:08:17.714 --> 00:08:22.018
He would find that
you've got for every two

00:08:22.118 --> 00:08:24.888
volumes of hydrogen gas--

00:08:24.988 --> 00:08:27.057
so you've got two
volumes of hydrogen

00:08:27.157 --> 00:08:34.764
that he would react with
one volume of oxygen.

00:08:34.865 --> 00:08:37.701
And he would notice
that he would get two

00:08:37.801 --> 00:08:40.971
volumes of something that had--

00:08:41.071 --> 00:08:42.205
let's not put a dash there--

00:08:42.304 --> 00:08:45.275
it had hydrogen
and oxygen in it.

00:08:45.375 --> 00:08:46.443
He knew that.

00:08:46.543 --> 00:08:49.579
Now, he knew this because
he was very careful.

00:08:49.679 --> 00:08:50.881
So nothing leaked.

00:08:50.981 --> 00:08:51.648
He said no.

00:08:51.748 --> 00:08:53.216
Everything is accounted for.

00:08:53.316 --> 00:08:56.786
Lavoisier holds--
conservation of mass.

00:08:56.887 --> 00:08:58.555
I'm not losing anything here.

00:08:58.655 --> 00:09:01.491
And as Lavoisier said,
in a chemical reaction,

00:09:01.591 --> 00:09:05.295
mass is neither
created nor destroyed.

00:09:05.395 --> 00:09:09.132
So I know that
whatever is over here

00:09:09.232 --> 00:09:11.768
must consist of
whatever I have here--

00:09:11.868 --> 00:09:16.439
oxygen and hydrogen.
And he kept doing it

00:09:16.540 --> 00:09:20.644
for other lots of
different types of gases.

00:09:20.744 --> 00:09:27.250
But it was very strange, because
what happens is sometimes,

00:09:27.350 --> 00:09:29.119
the volumes are the same.

00:09:29.219 --> 00:09:31.221
Like he did it for
hydrogen and chlorine.

00:09:31.321 --> 00:09:36.960
One volume of hydrogen,
and one volume of chlorine

00:09:37.060 --> 00:09:41.298
would give you two volumes
of hydrogen and chlorine--

00:09:41.398 --> 00:09:44.634
something that had hydrogen
and chlorine in it.

00:09:44.734 --> 00:09:48.071
And so there, you'd mix
one and add another one.

00:09:48.171 --> 00:09:49.105
And you'd get two.

00:09:49.205 --> 00:09:50.540
It seems it would
kind of makes sense.

00:09:50.640 --> 00:09:52.342
You add one volume.

00:09:52.442 --> 00:09:57.514
But here, you'd mix two
volumes, add another volume.

00:09:57.614 --> 00:10:00.984
And it would feel
like it was reduced.

00:10:01.084 --> 00:10:03.753
Something in the
reaction happened

00:10:03.853 --> 00:10:06.389
that gave you a lower volume.

00:10:06.489 --> 00:10:08.191
What could it possibly be?

00:10:08.291 --> 00:10:09.826
Right, what was going on.

00:10:09.926 --> 00:10:11.528
This is what they
were grappling with.

00:10:11.628 --> 00:10:13.597
This is where they were.

00:10:13.697 --> 00:10:19.536
And then along comes
[sighs] Avogadro.

00:10:19.636 --> 00:10:25.075
Now, I wish we all called him
Lorenzo Romano Amedeo Carlo

00:10:25.175 --> 00:10:27.911
Avogadro which is his full name.

00:10:28.011 --> 00:10:31.448
But we shall call him Avogadro.

00:10:31.548 --> 00:10:36.620
And he made a critical proposal.

00:10:36.720 --> 00:10:41.057
He said, look, the
way to explain what's

00:10:41.157 --> 00:10:45.528
going on in Gay-Lussac's
volume measurements

00:10:45.629 --> 00:10:50.165
is to make the following
assumption-- oh, I

00:10:50.266 --> 00:10:52.202
have another board
over here-- is to make

00:10:52.302 --> 00:10:54.371
the following assumption.

00:10:54.471 --> 00:10:57.574
And I think if you've had
high school chemistry,

00:10:57.674 --> 00:10:59.075
you know what this is.

00:10:59.175 --> 00:11:02.846
But I'm going to put it here,
because of how important it is.

00:11:02.946 --> 00:11:09.753
So Avogadro said that
at constant temperature

00:11:09.853 --> 00:11:23.566
and pressure equal volume
equals same number of particles.

00:11:28.204 --> 00:11:30.173
So if I have the
[? efomethesine ?] temperature

00:11:30.273 --> 00:11:32.709
[inaudible],, if I
have the same volume,

00:11:32.809 --> 00:11:34.444
I have the same number
of [? particles. ?]

00:11:34.544 --> 00:11:35.712
Now, what are those particles?

00:11:35.812 --> 00:11:37.313
Oh, he was flexible.

00:11:37.414 --> 00:11:38.114
This is important.

00:11:38.214 --> 00:11:39.115
He was flexible.

00:11:39.215 --> 00:11:40.283
He said, those particles
could be atoms,

00:11:40.383 --> 00:11:42.318
or they could be
combinations of atoms.

00:11:42.419 --> 00:11:44.287
They could be molecules.

00:11:44.387 --> 00:11:46.456
Remember, at the
time, they hadn't all

00:11:46.556 --> 00:11:49.759
settled on the atom
as a name, even.

00:11:49.859 --> 00:11:52.095
What were these things?

00:11:52.195 --> 00:11:54.230
Equal volume, same
number of particles.

00:11:54.330 --> 00:11:57.267
And this really helped,
because you see,

00:11:57.367 --> 00:12:03.373
if you make that assumption,
and you believe these volume

00:12:03.473 --> 00:12:07.911
measurements, then it becomes
very clear what you have there.

00:12:08.011 --> 00:12:10.447
Right?

00:12:10.547 --> 00:12:13.450
If I have the same number
of particles in here,

00:12:13.550 --> 00:12:16.019
and I believe in Lavoisier,
who tells me nothing

00:12:16.119 --> 00:12:17.787
comes in or out in
terms of the math,

00:12:17.887 --> 00:12:19.155
I didn't create
or destroy atoms,

00:12:19.255 --> 00:12:23.426
then you know that if I
have two volumes of water,

00:12:23.526 --> 00:12:28.364
let's say I started
with o and h.

00:12:28.465 --> 00:12:29.532
It doesn't work.

00:12:29.632 --> 00:12:33.103
I can't make enough water.

00:12:33.203 --> 00:12:35.038
I just can't make
enough particles.

00:12:35.138 --> 00:12:37.173
The number of
particles on the right

00:12:37.273 --> 00:12:40.744
has to be equal to the
number of particles

00:12:40.844 --> 00:12:43.880
in those two volumes of
hydrogen. It's got to add up.

00:12:43.980 --> 00:12:46.850
Counting has to work.

00:12:46.950 --> 00:12:51.354
That's what Avogadro gave
us is this idea of counting.

00:12:51.454 --> 00:12:53.823
And that's why we've named
a constant after him.

00:12:53.923 --> 00:12:54.991
So I'll get to--

00:12:55.091 --> 00:13:00.163
Gesundheit or in honor
of Avogadro, "salu-tay".

00:13:00.263 --> 00:13:02.165
[laughter]

00:13:02.265 --> 00:13:02.899
He was Italian.

00:13:05.602 --> 00:13:10.140
And so if you believe Avogadro,
if you believe Avogadro,

00:13:10.240 --> 00:13:15.078
this becomes clear, because
the only way for these things

00:13:15.178 --> 00:13:19.282
to work then is if
here I'm counting

00:13:19.382 --> 00:13:20.817
how much volume's here.

00:13:20.917 --> 00:13:25.421
So here I got HCL only if this
is a two, and that's a two--

00:13:25.522 --> 00:13:26.356
diatomic gases.

00:13:29.526 --> 00:13:31.227
That's the only way
the numbers work.

00:13:31.327 --> 00:13:34.664
And here, I've got
to have two and two.

00:13:34.764 --> 00:13:36.599
And this must be H2O.

00:13:36.699 --> 00:13:41.171
Oh, or at least it's one
simple and very plausible

00:13:41.271 --> 00:13:46.576
explanation if Avogadro's
hypothesis was right.

00:13:46.676 --> 00:13:48.845
Dalton didn't buy it.

00:13:48.945 --> 00:13:51.614
Dalton said, uh, uh, no way.

00:13:51.714 --> 00:13:53.183
You've got leeks, dude.

00:13:53.283 --> 00:13:55.852
And he really was harsh.

00:13:58.655 --> 00:14:02.592
Everybody thought water
was OH at the time.

00:14:02.692 --> 00:14:05.061
And so Dalton said, you
must have made a mistake.

00:14:05.161 --> 00:14:07.096
I block you on Instagram.

00:14:07.197 --> 00:14:08.464
[laughter]

00:14:08.565 --> 00:14:10.533
I block you.

00:14:10.633 --> 00:14:13.803
And it was pretty
tough, because Avogadro

00:14:13.903 --> 00:14:15.638
put a lot of really
brilliant work

00:14:15.738 --> 00:14:19.442
out there that was really
ignored for 50 years

00:14:19.542 --> 00:14:23.146
until Cannizzaro came
along and others.

00:14:23.246 --> 00:14:25.548
50 years later, two
years after he died

00:14:25.648 --> 00:14:28.852
was when we had
the first evidence

00:14:28.952 --> 00:14:31.454
that the scientific community
was willing to believe

00:14:31.554 --> 00:14:33.423
that his hypothesis was right.

00:14:33.523 --> 00:14:35.892
What a big deal his work was.

00:14:40.029 --> 00:14:40.730
OK.

00:14:40.830 --> 00:14:42.332
Now, I mentioned that this is--

00:14:42.432 --> 00:14:44.234
I'm mentioning all
this because we

00:14:44.334 --> 00:14:48.738
are going to use his constant
a lot, the Avogadro constant.

00:14:48.838 --> 00:14:50.707
And it's a way--

00:14:50.807 --> 00:14:52.809
and so I want to talk
about that constant--

00:14:52.909 --> 00:14:55.712
and I want to do it in
the context of our candle.

00:14:55.812 --> 00:14:59.382
And so because this
constant, this number,

00:14:59.482 --> 00:15:02.585
this idea of the
number of particles

00:15:02.685 --> 00:15:09.859
you have in chemistry is the
link between the atomic world

00:15:09.959 --> 00:15:11.427
and the macroscopic world.

00:15:11.527 --> 00:15:13.963
That's what his
constant does for us.

00:15:14.063 --> 00:15:14.898
OK?

00:15:14.998 --> 00:15:16.399
Now, let's put
that into context.

00:15:16.499 --> 00:15:22.138
If I were to ask you a question
like this, I have that candle.

00:15:22.238 --> 00:15:24.607
Remember, the
candle, the C25H52.

00:15:27.343 --> 00:15:30.146
And I say, well, OK,
you've lit the candle.

00:15:30.246 --> 00:15:32.448
How many candle molecules
burn in a minute?

00:15:32.548 --> 00:15:33.583
I want to know that.

00:15:33.683 --> 00:15:35.118
Don't we all?

00:15:35.218 --> 00:15:36.085
Right?

00:15:36.185 --> 00:15:37.487
That's a pretty
important question.

00:15:37.587 --> 00:15:38.821
Why are people nodding?

00:15:38.922 --> 00:15:41.257
Thank you for that agreement.

00:15:41.357 --> 00:15:43.393
And I could say, well, I
assume the 12-hour candle

00:15:43.493 --> 00:15:44.928
of the mass and 90
grams, let's get

00:15:45.028 --> 00:15:47.630
that off of a candle on Amazon.

00:15:47.730 --> 00:15:48.631
Right?

00:15:48.731 --> 00:15:50.466
How many molecules
burn in a minute?

00:15:50.566 --> 00:15:52.635
What I need to know
is, well, how many

00:15:52.735 --> 00:15:53.870
are we sort of talking about?

00:15:53.970 --> 00:15:56.773
How do we know how to
even answer this question?

00:15:56.873 --> 00:15:58.441
And it comes from the link.

00:15:58.540 --> 00:16:01.177
But see, to answer
this kind of question,

00:16:01.277 --> 00:16:03.913
which I'm sure so many people
wanted to know back then,

00:16:04.013 --> 00:16:08.318
you must have a way to
talk about big numbers.

00:16:08.418 --> 00:16:09.585
You must.

00:16:09.686 --> 00:16:10.987
Why?

00:16:11.087 --> 00:16:14.757
Because there's a lot of these
molecules in a very tiny amount

00:16:14.857 --> 00:16:15.792
of volume--

00:16:15.892 --> 00:16:18.161
a lot.

00:16:18.261 --> 00:16:22.832
Drop of water for perspective
has 10 trillion molecules--

00:16:22.932 --> 00:16:25.635
10 trillion molecules.

00:16:25.735 --> 00:16:29.005
Anybody know how many cells
you have in your body?

00:16:29.105 --> 00:16:31.741
600 trillion?

00:16:31.841 --> 00:16:34.377
Let's call it 100
trillion just to be nice.

00:16:34.477 --> 00:16:35.144
Right?

00:16:35.244 --> 00:16:38.047
It's a 100 trillion cells.

00:16:38.147 --> 00:16:41.584
And so if you're going to talk
about how many cells you have,

00:16:41.684 --> 00:16:44.354
or how many molecules
are in a drop of water,

00:16:44.454 --> 00:16:48.257
you need to have a big
number, a big scale.

00:16:48.358 --> 00:16:50.593
And that is what a mole is.

00:16:53.563 --> 00:16:55.531
Avogadro didn't come
up with this number.

00:16:55.631 --> 00:16:58.101
This was gotten
much later, right?

00:16:58.201 --> 00:17:00.269
But we name it
after him in honor

00:17:00.370 --> 00:17:05.208
of the work he did on
counting atoms and molecules.

00:17:05.308 --> 00:17:06.342
And that's the number.

00:17:06.442 --> 00:17:08.544
This is the way
we talk about it.

00:17:08.644 --> 00:17:12.949
And just to be complete,
Avogadro's constant,

00:17:13.049 --> 00:17:22.090
which is a mole, one mole
is equal to 6.022 times 10

00:17:22.191 --> 00:17:23.826
to the 23rd.

00:17:23.925 --> 00:17:26.362
It's just a number.

00:17:26.462 --> 00:17:27.030
All right?

00:17:27.130 --> 00:17:28.865
It's just a number.

00:17:28.965 --> 00:17:32.301
But it's a really important one
because it provides the link.

00:17:36.572 --> 00:17:41.144
OK, if I take like how many
eggs are there in a dozen eggs.

00:17:41.244 --> 00:17:42.478
It's 12.

00:17:42.578 --> 00:17:46.049
How many bags of sugar are
there in a dozen bags of sugar?

00:17:46.149 --> 00:17:47.550
It's 12.

00:17:47.650 --> 00:17:48.818
All right?

00:17:48.918 --> 00:17:52.221
How many atoms are there
in a mole of atoms?

00:17:52.321 --> 00:17:54.657
That number.

00:17:54.757 --> 00:17:55.425
That's all it is.

00:17:55.558 --> 00:17:56.793
It's a number.

00:17:56.893 --> 00:17:57.994
OK?

00:17:58.094 --> 00:18:02.398
Oh, but a dozen eggs
weighs differently

00:18:02.498 --> 00:18:04.634
than a dozen bags of sugar.

00:18:04.734 --> 00:18:08.204
So a mole of one atom will
have a different amount

00:18:08.304 --> 00:18:12.475
of mass, a different weight
than a mole of another atom.

00:18:12.575 --> 00:18:15.344
And that's the link
that he gave us.

00:18:15.445 --> 00:18:17.580
That's the link that
this number gave us.

00:18:17.680 --> 00:18:21.918
And, in particular, again, going
back to this idea of counting,

00:18:22.018 --> 00:18:26.923
we can count now with this link.

00:18:27.023 --> 00:18:29.325
If I said, how many
pennies are there?

00:18:29.425 --> 00:18:32.361
And I gave you a mass
of pennies, right?

00:18:32.462 --> 00:18:36.632
If I had 2.5
kilograms of pennies,

00:18:36.732 --> 00:18:39.102
and I say how many
pennies do I have?

00:18:39.202 --> 00:18:42.071
Then all I need to know is
how much each penny weighs.

00:18:42.171 --> 00:18:43.906
I'm not going to count them.

00:18:44.006 --> 00:18:45.408
Right, you don't
want to count them.

00:18:45.508 --> 00:18:47.810
You want to do something
a little easier.

00:18:47.910 --> 00:18:54.016
So if I have one penny,
weighs 2.5 grams.

00:18:54.117 --> 00:18:57.386
Then I know that I can just
get this really easily.

00:18:57.487 --> 00:19:01.057
I have 1,000 pennies.

00:19:01.157 --> 00:19:03.126
OK.

00:19:03.226 --> 00:19:04.760
What a mole does--

00:19:04.861 --> 00:19:06.629
a mole gives us the link.

00:19:06.729 --> 00:19:11.734
So a mole, if I
have moles, then I

00:19:11.834 --> 00:19:16.506
can go to the mass of an atom--

00:19:16.606 --> 00:19:19.442
the mass of an atom.

00:19:19.542 --> 00:19:23.513
And from that, I can go
all the way up to grams.

00:19:26.382 --> 00:19:28.384
And this is critical.

00:19:28.484 --> 00:19:29.485
OK.

00:19:29.585 --> 00:19:34.223
The mass of the atom
and the grams per mole

00:19:34.323 --> 00:19:35.391
are the same number.

00:19:35.491 --> 00:19:38.828
That's what the mole gives us.

00:19:38.928 --> 00:19:41.297
Right.

00:19:41.397 --> 00:19:47.703
So in the case of
carbon, carbon has

00:19:47.803 --> 00:19:54.310
a mass per atom of 12.011 AMU.

00:19:54.410 --> 00:19:58.848
These are atomic mass units.

00:19:58.948 --> 00:20:01.050
It's just a way
to measure things

00:20:01.150 --> 00:20:03.052
that have very little mass.

00:20:03.152 --> 00:20:06.589
But the link here is that if
I have a mole of these things,

00:20:06.689 --> 00:20:12.628
if I have a mole, one
mole of carbon, then

00:20:12.728 --> 00:20:19.502
I have exactly 12.011 grams.

00:20:19.602 --> 00:20:22.104
Back and forth, grams, atoms.

00:20:22.205 --> 00:20:25.908
Numbers of atoms, mass per atom.

00:20:26.008 --> 00:20:28.177
All right, so one
carbon atom has

00:20:28.277 --> 00:20:31.681
a mass of 12 atomic mass units.

00:20:31.781 --> 00:20:33.583
We don't need to know.

00:20:33.683 --> 00:20:36.919
This comes from weighing
neutrons and protons.

00:20:37.019 --> 00:20:39.889
All right, we'll get
to that next week.

00:20:39.989 --> 00:20:41.157
We don't even know that yet.

00:20:41.257 --> 00:20:45.728
But this is a mass of things
that the very tiny scale.

00:20:45.828 --> 00:20:49.765
And if I have a mole of
those atoms, I get to grams.

00:20:49.865 --> 00:20:50.633
OK?

00:20:50.733 --> 00:20:51.701
So it's my link.

00:20:51.801 --> 00:20:54.503
Now, where is this amount?

00:20:54.604 --> 00:20:58.107
Where is this
critical number that

00:20:58.207 --> 00:21:00.743
gives me the counting per gram?

00:21:00.843 --> 00:21:05.715
It's, of course, here
in your periodic table.

00:21:05.815 --> 00:21:08.184
This is where you
get that information.

00:21:08.284 --> 00:21:12.255
OK, so we'll be building
that up as we go.

00:21:12.355 --> 00:21:15.591
Well, let's say why
do I need to count?

00:21:15.691 --> 00:21:18.327
Well, let's go back to
answer our question now.

00:21:18.427 --> 00:21:20.062
That's the link.

00:21:20.162 --> 00:21:25.468
That number, Avogadro's constant
links these atomic masses

00:21:25.568 --> 00:21:26.702
to grams.

00:21:26.802 --> 00:21:27.436
All right?

00:21:30.406 --> 00:21:31.774
Now I can answer the question.

00:21:31.874 --> 00:21:34.543
How many candle molecules
burn in one minute?

00:21:34.644 --> 00:21:35.645
Well, let's do that.

00:21:35.745 --> 00:21:44.654
Because now I know
that one mole of C25H52

00:21:44.754 --> 00:21:53.129
is something like 352.7 grams.

00:21:53.229 --> 00:21:54.430
How do I know that?

00:21:54.530 --> 00:21:58.801
Because I can look
up what a mole

00:21:58.901 --> 00:22:01.837
weighs of carbon in
here, how many grams.

00:22:01.937 --> 00:22:05.174
And I can look up how many
grams of mole hydrogen weighs.

00:22:05.274 --> 00:22:08.077
And then I can
multiply 25 and 52.

00:22:08.177 --> 00:22:09.712
And that's how many grams I get.

00:22:09.812 --> 00:22:12.048
One mole of it is that.

00:22:12.148 --> 00:22:13.816
Aha, the link is coming.

00:22:13.916 --> 00:22:14.650
The link is coming.

00:22:14.750 --> 00:22:15.618
It's very exciting.

00:22:19.121 --> 00:22:24.093
Because in a 12-hour
candle, if it's 90 grams,

00:22:24.193 --> 00:22:31.901
so if I have a 90 gram candle,
as I've said in the problem,

00:22:32.001 --> 00:22:41.677
then I know now that
that is about 0.25 moles.

00:22:41.777 --> 00:22:43.813
Right, because of this.

00:22:43.913 --> 00:22:44.413
See that?

00:22:44.513 --> 00:22:46.015
90 over that.

00:22:46.115 --> 00:22:50.319
So now, I know how many
moles of candle I've got.

00:22:50.419 --> 00:22:52.688
I know how many moles
of candle I've got.

00:22:52.788 --> 00:22:57.626
But remember a mole
is just a number.

00:22:57.727 --> 00:23:00.062
It's just a number.

00:23:00.162 --> 00:23:03.099
So this is how many moles
of candle molecules I have.

00:23:03.199 --> 00:23:04.834
And OK.

00:23:04.934 --> 00:23:05.601
In one minute.

00:23:05.701 --> 00:23:06.869
It says in one minute.

00:23:06.969 --> 00:23:09.939
So in one minute--

00:23:10.039 --> 00:23:12.274
well, OK, there's 60
minutes in an hour,

00:23:12.375 --> 00:23:15.778
and it's 12 hours, just
a 720-minute candle.

00:23:15.878 --> 00:23:16.846
Right?

00:23:16.946 --> 00:23:21.417
So in one minute,
1/720 of that is what

00:23:21.517 --> 00:23:24.253
burns, if it lasts 12 hours.

00:23:24.353 --> 00:23:26.956
I'm just taking the
information I was given.

00:23:27.056 --> 00:23:30.426
And I'm using the math
of minutes and hours.

00:23:30.526 --> 00:23:33.396
1/720, OK.

00:23:33.496 --> 00:23:34.296
All right, good.

00:23:34.397 --> 00:23:38.000
So 1/720th of the candle burns.

00:23:38.100 --> 00:23:43.139
In one minute, 1/720
of the candle burns.

00:23:43.239 --> 00:23:47.309
And if the candle is that many
moles, that's this many moles--

00:23:47.410 --> 00:23:50.613
0.003.

00:23:50.713 --> 00:23:55.384
No, 0347 moles.

00:23:55.484 --> 00:23:56.752
Why did I do all that?

00:23:56.852 --> 00:23:59.622
Because I can now answer the
question, cause of Avogadro's

00:23:59.722 --> 00:24:02.491
constant, because a
mole is just a number.

00:24:02.591 --> 00:24:03.526
A mole is just a number.

00:24:03.626 --> 00:24:06.262
So if I've got that
many moles, then I

00:24:06.362 --> 00:24:18.741
know that I've got 2 times 10
to the 20th molecules roughly.

00:24:18.841 --> 00:24:20.509
So I have been able to
answer this question

00:24:20.609 --> 00:24:21.777
by going back and forth.

00:24:21.877 --> 00:24:22.812
[inaudible]

00:24:22.912 --> 00:24:25.281
I look here to get
the grams per mole.

00:24:25.381 --> 00:24:28.584
I figured out how
much candle I had.

00:24:28.684 --> 00:24:31.320
In grams, that gave me how
many moles I have of candle.

00:24:31.420 --> 00:24:32.888
And then gave me the number--

00:24:32.988 --> 00:24:34.156
back and forth, back and forth.

00:24:34.256 --> 00:24:35.524
That's what we do.

00:24:35.624 --> 00:24:40.229
Avogadro's constant gets us in
and out of the atomic world.

00:24:40.329 --> 00:24:41.464
OK?

00:24:41.564 --> 00:24:42.832
And so we're going to
practice that a little bit.

00:24:45.568 --> 00:24:49.538
And this gets me back to
what we started on Wednesday.

00:24:49.638 --> 00:24:53.142
And I always like to give
