00:28:34.229 --> 00:28:38.300
became easy to do
or a lot easier.

00:28:38.400 --> 00:28:39.768
OK?

00:28:39.868 --> 00:28:41.537
And the process
that enabled that

00:28:41.637 --> 00:28:45.340
is called the
Haber-Bosch process.

00:28:45.441 --> 00:28:49.878
But it is what allowed
us to feed billions

00:28:49.978 --> 00:28:53.615
of people in a sustainable way.

00:28:53.716 --> 00:28:55.818
It's arguable whether we're
doing that sustainably

00:28:55.918 --> 00:28:58.320
but in a way where we could
actually produce enough--

00:28:58.420 --> 00:28:59.488
enough what?

00:28:59.588 --> 00:29:00.823
Enough ammonia.

00:29:00.923 --> 00:29:06.462
And here's the deal you see,
because plants need nitrogen

00:29:06.562 --> 00:29:08.464
to grow.

00:29:08.564 --> 00:29:09.965
Right?

00:29:10.065 --> 00:29:12.768
And there's 70% of the
atmosphere is nitrogen.

00:29:12.868 --> 00:29:15.604
But it's useless,
because it's N2.

00:29:15.704 --> 00:29:18.173
And N2 is one of the
strongest bonds in nature.

00:29:18.273 --> 00:29:20.209
And plants can't break it.

00:29:20.309 --> 00:29:23.212
Now, we knew how
to break it before.

00:29:23.312 --> 00:29:26.181
We knew how to do
this before, right?

00:29:26.281 --> 00:29:28.150
This is called fixing--

00:29:28.250 --> 00:29:35.357
fixing nitrogen. And the way
it works is you go N2 plus h.

00:29:35.457 --> 00:29:39.528
This is a reaction, goes to NH3.

00:29:39.628 --> 00:29:40.863
What is wrong with this?

00:29:40.963 --> 00:29:41.864
[inaudible]

00:29:41.964 --> 00:29:44.466
It's not balanced.

00:29:44.566 --> 00:29:45.701
Thank you.

00:29:45.801 --> 00:29:50.539
Did anybody say, balancing
is the same as mole ratio.

00:29:50.639 --> 00:29:54.209
Balancing and mole ratio's
the same-- counting.

00:29:54.309 --> 00:29:55.410
Remember, we talk about that.

00:29:55.511 --> 00:29:57.112
But balancing is counting.

00:29:57.212 --> 00:29:58.847
So let's see-- oh, 2 here.

00:29:58.947 --> 00:30:01.917
OK, that helps me
with the nitrogen.

00:30:02.017 --> 00:30:03.152
Oh, but this is H2.

00:30:03.252 --> 00:30:04.753
I meant to put that
in the first place.

00:30:04.853 --> 00:30:07.189
And then so this is a 3.

00:30:07.289 --> 00:30:09.158
That reaction was
known and doable.

00:30:09.291 --> 00:30:12.294
But it took tremendous
amounts of energy.

00:30:12.394 --> 00:30:13.629
So it's very difficult to scale.

00:30:13.729 --> 00:30:16.632
And Haber-Bosch came up
with a way using catalysis

00:30:16.732 --> 00:30:18.066
to do it at much
lower temperatures.

00:30:18.167 --> 00:30:20.469
Catalysis is something we'll
learn later in the semester.

00:30:23.338 --> 00:30:25.407
But how do we answer
this question?

00:30:25.507 --> 00:30:26.475
What's my question?

00:30:26.575 --> 00:30:29.411
My question is how
long can we keep going?

00:30:29.511 --> 00:30:34.082
50% of every protein
you put in your body--

00:30:34.183 --> 00:30:36.819
50% come from this--

00:30:36.919 --> 00:30:40.022
comes from some plant that
was grown using this process.

00:30:40.122 --> 00:30:42.157
That's how important it is.

00:30:42.257 --> 00:30:46.161
It's 500 million
tons of nitrogen

00:30:46.261 --> 00:30:48.831
is made this way every year.

00:30:48.931 --> 00:30:50.799
Well, it's just counting.

00:30:50.899 --> 00:30:58.006
One mole of ammonia is 17 grams.

00:30:58.106 --> 00:30:59.508
Oh, we're doing the same thing.

00:30:59.608 --> 00:31:00.475
How did I know that?

00:31:00.576 --> 00:31:02.010
Periodic table.

00:31:02.110 --> 00:31:03.979
That's how I knew that.

00:31:04.079 --> 00:31:07.049
One mole-- if that many
molecules of ammonia,

00:31:07.149 --> 00:31:08.383
I've got 17 grams of it.

00:31:14.323 --> 00:31:18.393
Let's say I just need to
make the same amount of--

00:31:18.493 --> 00:31:22.531
it says so times
10 to the 6 tons.

00:31:22.631 --> 00:31:29.538
I need that much
tons of NH3 per year.

00:31:29.638 --> 00:31:31.006
That's our-- yeah, per year--

00:31:31.106 --> 00:31:32.140
per year.

00:31:32.241 --> 00:31:35.143
OK, so that sets up my problem.

00:31:35.244 --> 00:31:37.212
I know how much I need.

00:31:37.312 --> 00:31:41.617
I know how many atoms we're
talking about in one mole.

00:31:41.717 --> 00:31:45.821
Now, I can actually
understand how many moles--

00:31:45.921 --> 00:31:48.523
so I'm just going to
not do the detailed math

00:31:48.624 --> 00:31:49.858
but how many moles I have.

00:31:49.958 --> 00:31:55.163
So I've got 30 times
10 to the 12th moles.

00:31:55.264 --> 00:32:03.739
This is how many moles
I needed per year.

00:32:03.839 --> 00:32:06.008
How much do I have?

00:32:06.108 --> 00:32:07.542
Well, we know how
much the atmosphere

00:32:07.643 --> 00:32:09.044
weighs-- it turns out.

00:32:09.144 --> 00:32:13.115
We know how much the
atmosphere weighs.

00:32:13.215 --> 00:32:20.889
The mass of the atmosphere
is something like 5 times 10

00:32:20.989 --> 00:32:23.191
to the 21st grams.

00:32:23.292 --> 00:32:35.637
And if I take 78% of that
as my N2, so 78% is N2,

00:32:35.737 --> 00:32:39.574
then I can tell
you that I've got--

00:32:39.675 --> 00:32:50.385
let's see, 1.4 times 10
to the 20th moles of N2.

00:32:50.485 --> 00:32:52.020
That's how much
I have available.

00:32:56.124 --> 00:32:57.259
OK?

00:32:57.359 --> 00:32:59.594
So if I keep on taking
N2 out of the atmosphere,

00:32:59.695 --> 00:33:02.197
then I can now answer the
question just like the candle.

00:33:02.297 --> 00:33:03.532
All right, how long do you have?

00:33:03.632 --> 00:33:05.334
I can answer the
question, how long can we

00:33:05.434 --> 00:33:10.172
keep taking N2 out
of the atmosphere

00:33:10.272 --> 00:33:13.775
and using Haber-Bosch, right?

00:33:13.875 --> 00:33:15.944
Oh, well, you would use
the balanced reaction.

00:33:16.044 --> 00:33:17.479
So for every mole
event do I take,

00:33:17.579 --> 00:33:19.348
I get two moles of ammonia.

00:33:19.448 --> 00:33:20.248
That's good.

00:33:20.349 --> 00:33:22.150
And you can work backwards.

00:33:22.250 --> 00:33:24.753
And you can learn
that you're good.

00:33:24.853 --> 00:33:28.924
We're good for now.

00:33:29.024 --> 00:33:31.026
We have roughly
100 million years.

00:33:34.062 --> 00:33:34.463
OK?

00:33:39.868 --> 00:33:42.671
We have 100 million years
that we could keep consuming.

00:33:42.771 --> 00:33:45.607
And then we'll run out
of N2 in the atmosphere.

00:33:45.707 --> 00:33:47.376
How fun was that?

00:33:47.476 --> 00:33:49.411
OK, I love doing this.

00:33:49.511 --> 00:33:52.114
Now, when I get excited--

00:33:52.214 --> 00:33:53.448
wow.

00:33:53.548 --> 00:33:56.651
Wait, before I get excited,
what's the limiting reagent?

00:33:56.752 --> 00:33:58.086
[? add ?] enough N2.

00:33:58.186 --> 00:33:59.321
Is it N2?

00:33:59.421 --> 00:34:01.156
How do you know?

00:34:01.256 --> 00:34:01.957
How could you show?

00:34:02.057 --> 00:34:04.659
How can you prove?

00:34:04.760 --> 00:34:06.962
Limiting range, it means
what runs out first?

00:34:07.062 --> 00:34:10.364
Well, oh, I'll just take H2
from the atmosphere, right?

00:34:10.465 --> 00:34:12.467
Na, uh.

00:34:12.567 --> 00:34:14.770
There's no H2 in the atmosphere.

00:34:14.870 --> 00:34:16.538
Where's the H2?

00:34:16.638 --> 00:34:18.907
Di da, it's over here--

00:34:19.007 --> 00:34:20.007
H2O.

00:34:20.108 --> 00:34:21.176
It's in the oceans.

00:34:21.275 --> 00:34:22.577
How much H2O do I have?

00:34:22.677 --> 00:34:23.078
Well--

00:34:23.178 --> 00:34:26.114
[laughter]

00:34:26.214 --> 00:34:28.583
It turns out you've got a lot.

00:34:28.683 --> 00:34:32.954
10 to the 23rd-ish
moles of H2O, right?

00:34:33.054 --> 00:34:34.623
10 to the 23rd-ish moles.

00:34:34.723 --> 00:34:37.592
And so because of those
coefficients of the reaction,

00:34:37.692 --> 00:34:40.228
you know exactly which one
is going to run out first.

00:34:40.328 --> 00:34:43.065
You know it's N2, so your
answer over there was correct.

00:34:43.165 --> 00:34:45.233
N2 is the limiting--

00:34:45.333 --> 00:34:46.301
I love that little dance.

00:34:46.400 --> 00:34:47.735
I'm going to try to learn that.

00:34:47.835 --> 00:34:50.405
I'm going to learn that later.

00:34:50.505 --> 00:34:52.706
If you try this, you
go 100 million years.

00:34:52.808 --> 00:34:56.043
You run out of N2--
plenty of H2 left.

00:34:56.143 --> 00:34:57.112
Let me read you.

00:34:57.212 --> 00:34:59.548
When I get excited,
I didn't miss this.

00:34:59.648 --> 00:35:02.050
I like to throw
stuff like T-shirts.

00:35:02.150 --> 00:35:06.521
Now, the thing is these
T-shirts are not--

00:35:06.621 --> 00:35:08.323
[laughter]

00:35:08.423 --> 00:35:10.192
Now, and I go like this.

00:35:10.292 --> 00:35:11.993
[laughter]

00:35:12.094 --> 00:35:17.499
And then I go all the way--
ahh, to the back, all the way

00:35:17.599 --> 00:35:21.670
up there, all the way up there.

00:35:21.770 --> 00:35:23.939
All right.

00:35:24.039 --> 00:35:30.579
OK, now look-- why
do I throw T-shirts?

00:35:30.679 --> 00:35:31.613
I have no idea.

00:35:31.713 --> 00:35:33.748
[laughter]

00:35:33.849 --> 00:35:37.686
Sometimes things are
just better left unknown.

00:35:37.786 --> 00:35:44.626
But please, I'll throw
T-shirts 34 more times.

00:35:44.726 --> 00:35:46.561
Every lecture, I'm going
to throw some T-shirts

00:35:46.661 --> 00:35:47.829
when I get a little excited.

00:35:47.929 --> 00:35:51.833
And please, if you get a
T-shirt a second time, share.

00:35:51.933 --> 00:35:54.769
Right, don't take it-- give
it to somebody-- trade.

00:35:54.870 --> 00:35:55.403
Right?

00:35:55.504 --> 00:35:56.705
Sharing is caring.

00:35:56.805 --> 00:35:57.506
Now, where were we.

00:35:57.606 --> 00:35:59.975
OK, now, here we are.

00:36:00.075 --> 00:36:01.877
We got to build this?

00:36:01.977 --> 00:36:08.250
Here's the thing-- in 1669,
there are only 12 elements

00:36:08.350 --> 00:36:09.518
known.

00:36:09.618 --> 00:36:11.586
But yeah, I hope I've
given you a sense.

00:36:11.686 --> 00:36:14.356
All these people and many
more, I haven't even named.

00:36:14.456 --> 00:36:18.260
We're working on
discovering new ones.

00:36:18.360 --> 00:36:23.398
By 1799, we had 34.

00:36:23.498 --> 00:36:25.600
Dalton and I mentioned
had a table of 36,

00:36:25.700 --> 00:36:27.202
and they kept going.

00:36:27.302 --> 00:36:31.373
And as we got more and more
elements, a lot of people

00:36:31.473 --> 00:36:34.276
were wondering, how
do we organize these?

00:36:34.376 --> 00:36:36.011
How do we organize these?

00:36:36.111 --> 00:36:37.679
And are there patterns?

00:36:37.779 --> 00:36:39.881
Are there patterns, right?

00:36:39.981 --> 00:36:41.816
That became a hot question.

00:36:41.917 --> 00:36:44.319
Yes, they still wanted to smash
and break and burn and make

00:36:44.419 --> 00:36:45.954
new, discover new things.

00:36:46.054 --> 00:36:48.590
But they also wanted to
know what it all means.

00:36:48.690 --> 00:36:51.560
It wasn't just like
a few-- it was a lot.

00:36:51.660 --> 00:36:53.328
And so they started
looking for patterns.

00:36:53.428 --> 00:36:55.997
And one of the first
people was Dobereiner,

00:36:56.097 --> 00:37:00.468
who came up with what he called
the Law of Triads, which was

00:37:00.569 --> 00:37:01.903
a law that worked three times.

00:37:02.003 --> 00:37:05.574
[laughs] But what he found--

00:37:05.674 --> 00:37:08.043
[inaudible],, everybody was
arranging these elements

00:37:08.143 --> 00:37:08.877
in different ways.

00:37:08.977 --> 00:37:11.146
[inaudible] arrange it by mass.

00:37:11.246 --> 00:37:13.582
No, arrange it by
something else.

00:37:13.682 --> 00:37:16.952
And he found that if you
listed him kind of by mass-ish,

00:37:17.052 --> 00:37:20.422
then the middle
atom of the three

00:37:20.522 --> 00:37:23.892
would weigh exactly half or
roughly half of these two.

00:37:23.992 --> 00:37:25.627
That was cool for three times.

00:37:28.363 --> 00:37:31.800
OK, Newlands did something
really important.

00:37:31.900 --> 00:37:35.604
Well, first of all,
he loved music.

00:37:35.704 --> 00:37:39.107
And he arranged elements
like this and again, kind

00:37:39.207 --> 00:37:40.508
of by mass.

00:37:40.609 --> 00:37:44.212
And he noticed that there were
repeating patterns every eight.

00:37:44.312 --> 00:37:46.514
Now, unlike a lot of triads,
this was really important,

00:37:46.615 --> 00:37:48.116
because the Law of
Triads, you said,

00:37:48.216 --> 00:37:49.584
look, I can take these three.

00:37:49.684 --> 00:37:50.919
I can take those three.

00:37:51.019 --> 00:37:54.623
That's kind of cool,
but it wasn't periodic.

00:37:54.723 --> 00:37:57.259
It didn't happen these
three then, the next three.

00:37:57.359 --> 00:38:00.996
And whereas the Newlands,
it was periodic.

00:38:01.096 --> 00:38:04.933
And it sort of also only
worked for 2 and 1/2 runs.

00:38:05.033 --> 00:38:06.601
The Law of Octaves--

00:38:06.701 --> 00:38:08.270
he called it the Law of Octaves.

00:38:08.370 --> 00:38:10.472
Oh, this was the time
to look for patterns.

00:38:10.572 --> 00:38:14.142
And many people
took a stab at it.

00:38:14.242 --> 00:38:17.545
Julius Meyer made some really
important contributions

00:38:17.646 --> 00:38:21.049
in measuring the molar volume
by knowing the densities

00:38:21.149 --> 00:38:22.951
and finding patterns.

00:38:23.051 --> 00:38:25.587
they were looking
for periodicity.

00:38:25.687 --> 00:38:27.389
But as many of you
may already know,

00:38:27.489 --> 00:38:34.596
it was Mendeleev who figured
out what needed to be done.

00:38:34.696 --> 00:38:39.067
And what he figured out
was absolutely critical.

00:38:39.167 --> 00:38:40.535
Let's go to the middle.

00:38:43.838 --> 00:38:45.240
And notice, he's listing them--

00:38:45.340 --> 00:38:47.909
OK, he didn't know whether to
put it this way or sideways

00:38:48.009 --> 00:38:50.011
or what.

00:38:50.111 --> 00:38:53.248
This is oriented differently
than what we do today.

00:38:53.348 --> 00:38:54.449
But it's OK.

00:38:54.549 --> 00:38:56.751
He's just listing them
and looking for patterns.

00:38:56.851 --> 00:39:00.388
But what Mendeleev did
that were so critical--

00:39:00.488 --> 00:39:04.759
what he did that
was so important

00:39:04.859 --> 00:39:08.630
is that he organized them.

00:39:08.730 --> 00:39:14.369
So Mendeleev-- Mendeleev--

00:39:14.469 --> 00:39:19.107
he had, at the time, this
was-- let's see 1869.

00:39:19.207 --> 00:39:21.176
He had 63 elements.

00:39:23.878 --> 00:39:27.582
And he called this
a periodic system.

00:39:27.682 --> 00:39:35.457
And the critical piece is
he arranged them by mass.

00:39:38.193 --> 00:39:41.830
And this is so
important-- properties.

00:39:46.501 --> 00:39:52.974
He really believed that the
periodicity was the driver,

00:39:53.074 --> 00:39:57.812
and that the properties were
critical in this organization--

00:39:57.912 --> 00:40:00.048
the properties and the mass.

00:40:00.148 --> 00:40:00.548
Right?

00:40:04.386 --> 00:40:11.092
He was so certain that you had
to take them both into account

00:40:11.192 --> 00:40:14.963
that he actually left gaps.

00:40:15.063 --> 00:40:17.499
That was new.

00:40:17.599 --> 00:40:20.735
At least for this many elements
and arranging it this way,

00:40:20.835 --> 00:40:21.836
that was different.

00:40:21.936 --> 00:40:23.738
And so like, for
example, look at this.

00:40:23.838 --> 00:40:29.644
Over here, so here you
have aluminum, silicon.

00:40:29.744 --> 00:40:30.612
And you know what else?

00:40:30.712 --> 00:40:31.780
Uncover this, so you can it.

00:40:31.880 --> 00:40:33.648
Oh, zinc-- OK, zinc.

00:40:33.748 --> 00:40:34.849
Right, arsenic.

00:40:34.949 --> 00:40:38.553
But he said, no, don't
just put this next to zinc

00:40:38.653 --> 00:40:40.755
because of the properties.

00:40:40.855 --> 00:40:42.390
This doesn't have
the right properties.

00:40:42.490 --> 00:40:44.492
This should be more
like phosphorus.

00:40:44.592 --> 00:40:45.693
So we're going to leave gaps.

00:40:48.830 --> 00:40:51.933
And this is really important--
he predicted the properties

00:40:52.033 --> 00:40:53.334
of those missing elements.

00:40:53.435 --> 00:40:56.571
So if you call this, for
example, with silicon,

00:40:56.671 --> 00:40:58.807
we know this is germanium.

00:40:58.907 --> 00:41:00.141
We know that now.

00:41:00.241 --> 00:41:01.876
But back then, no one had
smashed and burned and broken

00:41:01.976 --> 00:41:05.346
enough stuff to find germanium.

00:41:05.447 --> 00:41:07.348
So he said, well,
we can predict it.

00:41:07.449 --> 00:41:10.885
Because it comes here in this
table in this organization,

00:41:10.985 --> 00:41:13.455
therefore, it must
have properties

00:41:13.555 --> 00:41:18.560
similar to other
elements in that column.

00:41:18.660 --> 00:41:19.861
And he called it eco-silicon.

00:41:19.961 --> 00:41:21.729
Eco just means one next, right?

00:41:21.830 --> 00:41:24.232
And so these are predictions
by Mendeleev-- predictions

00:41:24.332 --> 00:41:26.334
of the properties of
this missing element.

00:41:26.434 --> 00:41:29.838
And here's what you get when you
actually find germanium years

00:41:29.938 --> 00:41:30.772
and years later.

00:41:30.872 --> 00:41:31.940
And you measure
those properties.

00:41:32.040 --> 00:41:33.107
And look at that--

00:41:33.208 --> 00:41:35.009
it's incredible.

00:41:35.109 --> 00:41:36.144
Right?

00:41:36.244 --> 00:41:39.481
Thank you for that wow
because I felt it too.

00:41:39.581 --> 00:41:40.582
Any more T-shirts?

00:41:40.682 --> 00:41:43.651
Aw, I felt the wow,
and it made me excited.

00:41:43.751 --> 00:41:46.154
[laughter]

00:41:46.254 --> 00:41:48.156
All right.

00:41:48.256 --> 00:41:48.990
This is a big deal.

00:41:49.090 --> 00:41:50.892
You go back to here--

00:41:50.992 --> 00:41:54.596
iodine and tellurium--
no, I'm not

00:41:54.696 --> 00:41:56.364
going to give you
the mass ordering,

00:41:56.464 --> 00:41:58.500
because it doesn't make
sense in terms of property.

00:41:58.566 --> 00:42:01.636
So I'm going to keep the mass
the other way, even though this

00:42:01.736 --> 00:42:03.872
has a higher mass, look
at that question mark.

00:42:03.972 --> 00:42:07.275
I'm still going to put it before
because it makes more sense.

00:42:07.375 --> 00:42:11.212
That's what Mendeleev did, and
it was absolutely critical.

00:42:11.312 --> 00:42:14.482
It really brings us to the
current system that we use.

00:42:14.582 --> 00:42:16.684
So this is now the
periodic table.

00:42:16.784 --> 00:42:21.356
Our periodic table today is
based Mendeleev's organization.

00:42:21.456 --> 00:42:24.325
And we're going to be spending
a lot of time with it.

00:42:24.425 --> 00:42:26.160
So we're not going to
go through everything.

00:42:26.261 --> 00:42:27.595
But a couple of things.

00:42:27.695 --> 00:42:30.031
First of all, there's a key.

00:42:30.131 --> 00:42:31.733
That's the key to the table.

00:42:31.833 --> 00:42:35.336
If a table doesn't have
the key, don't use it.

00:42:35.436 --> 00:42:37.138
It should have a key.

00:42:37.238 --> 00:42:41.175
And so, for example,
oh, look at that number.

00:42:41.276 --> 00:42:42.210
That looks nice.

00:42:42.310 --> 00:42:43.444
Right?

00:42:43.545 --> 00:42:46.548
That's the AMU or the
grams per mole which we now

00:42:46.648 --> 00:42:48.016
know are equivalent--

00:42:48.116 --> 00:42:51.052
not equivalent but relatable.

00:42:51.152 --> 00:42:53.755
You have a mole, that's
how many grams you have.

00:42:53.855 --> 00:42:56.291
Or if you have one atom,
that's how many AMU's you have,

00:42:56.391 --> 00:42:57.258
right there.

00:42:57.358 --> 00:42:59.794
The key tells you
what things are.

00:42:59.894 --> 00:43:01.563
Right?

00:43:01.663 --> 00:43:05.300
Now, if you have a more
complicated one, don't panic.

00:43:05.400 --> 00:43:06.367
Here's yours.

00:43:06.467 --> 00:43:08.803
This is your periodic table.

00:43:08.903 --> 00:43:10.772
Ha, look at this.

00:43:10.872 --> 00:43:13.441
It's all in the key.

00:43:13.541 --> 00:43:17.445
There's a lot more information
in here then in the basic ones.

00:43:17.545 --> 00:43:18.580
But it's all in the key.

00:43:18.680 --> 00:43:19.881
It's all described in the key.

00:43:19.981 --> 00:43:23.051
And the key may be different
on the front and the back.

00:43:23.151 --> 00:43:25.887
There may be different
properties included.

00:43:25.987 --> 00:43:26.955
But it's all in the key.

00:43:27.055 --> 00:43:28.022
And look at it.

00:43:28.122 --> 00:43:29.657
Some of these we know
like the weight--

00:43:29.757 --> 00:43:31.960
atomic weight-- that's what
we've been talking about.

00:43:32.060 --> 00:43:34.128
Melting point sounds kind
of obvious-- boiling point

00:43:34.228 --> 00:43:35.496
density.

00:43:35.597 --> 00:43:37.365
And then you get to these other
things-- electronegativity,

00:43:37.465 --> 00:43:38.666
first ionization.

00:43:38.766 --> 00:43:40.835
All these things, what are they?

00:43:40.935 --> 00:43:44.939
We will find out literally
next week, all right,

00:43:45.039 --> 00:43:48.042
as we build up our
understanding of the atom.

00:43:48.142 --> 00:43:49.177
But it's all on the key.

00:43:49.277 --> 00:43:52.447
Now, this is so
important that I really

00:43:52.547 --> 00:43:58.319
ask you with passion to carry
it with you everywhere you go.

00:43:58.419 --> 00:44:00.521
I really do, because
you never know.

00:44:00.622 --> 00:44:02.323
Remember the restaurant example.

00:44:02.423 --> 00:44:04.826
You never know when you need it.

00:44:04.926 --> 00:44:06.027
You never know.

00:44:06.127 --> 00:44:08.262
And, in fact, you
can send me pictures.

00:44:08.363 --> 00:44:08.963
Students do this.

00:44:09.063 --> 00:44:09.964
I love it.

00:44:10.064 --> 00:44:11.432
You don't have to
send me pictures.

00:44:11.532 --> 00:44:14.202
You can just post it on your
feed or whatever it's called.

00:44:14.302 --> 00:44:15.203
[inaudible]

00:44:15.303 --> 00:44:16.004
What's it called?

00:44:16.104 --> 00:44:19.540
I don't know-- a thing.

00:44:19.641 --> 00:44:21.376
So they took it all the
way up to Dartmouth,

00:44:21.476 --> 00:44:23.611
because there were some people
up there apparently that needed

00:44:23.711 --> 00:44:24.679
help with their chemistry.

00:44:24.779 --> 00:44:26.347
[laughter]

00:44:26.447 --> 00:44:29.817
Here we are at a restaurant,
looking at the periodic table.

00:44:29.917 --> 00:44:30.918
Here's another.

00:44:31.019 --> 00:44:32.787
It's a good thing to
have at a restaurant.

00:44:32.887 --> 00:44:34.756
You never know.

00:44:34.856 --> 00:44:36.591
Here it is again.

00:44:36.691 --> 00:44:38.292
Look at how happy they are.

00:44:38.393 --> 00:44:40.428
Look at that, right?

00:44:40.528 --> 00:44:41.796
They're really getting into it.

00:44:41.896 --> 00:44:47.802
They're really getting into it
here, teaching family members

00:44:47.902 --> 00:44:50.238
about this or being proud
of it with the whole family.

00:44:50.338 --> 00:44:52.974
[laughter]

00:44:53.074 --> 00:44:55.443
Take it with you.

00:44:55.543 --> 00:44:57.311
It'll come in handy
no matter what.

00:44:57.412 --> 00:44:59.814
Here it is under
a Christmas tree.

00:44:59.914 --> 00:45:01.049
Right?

00:45:01.149 --> 00:45:02.917
It's in here somewhere.

00:45:03.017 --> 00:45:04.118
And there it is.

00:45:04.218 --> 00:45:07.288
And look, even if you're
dressing up for a theater,

00:45:07.388 --> 00:45:08.956
it's going to be important.

00:45:09.057 --> 00:45:11.726
OK, now, this is the
one I want to get to--

00:45:11.826 --> 00:45:13.261
OK, even if you're
a little tired.

00:45:13.361 --> 00:45:14.762
It's going to be
what comforts you.

00:45:14.862 --> 00:45:17.665
I think it's being
used as a pillow.

00:45:17.765 --> 00:45:20.702
That's OK, it's comfortable.

00:45:20.802 --> 00:45:21.235
Look at this.

00:45:21.335 --> 00:45:24.739
[laughter]

00:45:26.340 --> 00:45:27.575
Right?

00:45:27.675 --> 00:45:28.910
Right?

00:45:29.010 --> 00:45:32.580
You know even at the dance
club, even at the dance club,

00:45:32.680 --> 00:45:33.948
oh, I see that.

00:45:34.048 --> 00:45:35.083
You might need this.

00:45:35.183 --> 00:45:36.884
You don't know.

00:45:36.984 --> 00:45:39.087
You don't know.

00:45:39.187 --> 00:45:40.655
Look at how happy he is.

00:45:40.755 --> 00:45:42.056
[laughter]

00:45:42.156 --> 00:45:46.527
Look at that,
because he has this.

00:45:46.627 --> 00:45:50.431
Take it with you
everywhere you go.

00:45:50.531 --> 00:45:52.633
Now, there's a bunch of
things about this table

00:45:52.734 --> 00:45:55.236
that we'll be talking about.

00:45:55.336 --> 00:45:56.204
We just built it up.

00:45:59.140 --> 00:46:01.375
The first things is
just some nomenclature.

00:46:01.476 --> 00:46:03.244
Right, just how
do we name things?

00:46:03.344 --> 00:46:07.248
We name things that the
rose, we call them periods,

00:46:07.348 --> 00:46:08.683
and there's seven of them.

00:46:08.783 --> 00:46:10.818
The cones, we call them
groups, and there's

00:46:10.918 --> 00:46:14.355
18 of them in the way
we organize, in the way

00:46:14.455 --> 00:46:16.758
we present it, right?

00:46:16.891 --> 00:46:18.426
But there's something
kind of important

00:46:18.526 --> 00:46:21.529
that happens here which is
that these rows down here are

00:46:21.629 --> 00:46:25.633
actually 14 elements longer.

00:46:25.733 --> 00:46:28.136
So right, so this is 18 across.

00:46:28.236 --> 00:46:30.705
But these guys
fit in right here.

00:46:30.805 --> 00:46:32.039
They go right there.

00:46:32.140 --> 00:46:33.374
Right?

00:46:33.474 --> 00:46:36.511
These are called the lanthanides
because that's lanthanum--

00:46:36.611 --> 00:46:37.779
innovative naming.

00:46:37.879 --> 00:46:39.447
These are called the actinides.

00:46:39.547 --> 00:46:40.248
OK.

00:46:40.348 --> 00:46:43.284
They go in there in between.

00:46:43.384 --> 00:46:44.786
Why don't we just
put it all there?

00:46:44.886 --> 00:46:46.020
Well, you can.

00:46:46.120 --> 00:46:49.457
But then you got the
bottom rows are 34.

00:46:49.557 --> 00:46:50.291
Right?

00:46:50.391 --> 00:46:51.425
And then the next one's up.

00:46:51.526 --> 00:46:53.060
There's only two rows
in the periodic table

00:46:53.161 --> 00:46:55.263
that are actually 18.

00:46:55.363 --> 00:46:57.932
Then because then
you got 18 here.

00:46:58.032 --> 00:46:59.801
And then these are eight.

00:46:59.901 --> 00:47:01.135
And then you got two up top.

00:47:01.235 --> 00:47:02.737
Now, why?

00:47:02.837 --> 00:47:05.306
That we will understand
when we understand the atom

00:47:05.406 --> 00:47:07.108
a little better next week.

00:47:07.208 --> 00:47:08.476
OK?

00:47:08.576 --> 00:47:10.244
But for now, I just want to
call your attention that these

00:47:10.344 --> 00:47:11.412
are the names that we use.

00:47:11.512 --> 00:47:13.447
And those top ones
are called mean group.

00:47:13.548 --> 00:47:15.917
These ones are
transition elements.

00:47:16.017 --> 00:47:17.018
You have alkalines here.

00:47:17.118 --> 00:47:19.887
You have lanthanides, actinides.

00:47:19.987 --> 00:47:22.223
These are the names that
we give these regions that

00:47:22.323 --> 00:47:26.427
get more clear when we
know what electrons are.

00:47:26.527 --> 00:47:30.364
And I think I'll tell
you just the last bits

00:47:30.464 --> 00:47:33.100
about just a couple
more observations

00:47:33.201 --> 00:47:35.736
about the periodic
table, I think,

00:47:35.837 --> 00:47:39.740
when we start our lecture
on Monday since it's 11:54.

00:47:39.841 --> 00:47:42.610
And as promised, I want to
let you guys out right on time

00:47:42.710 --> 00:47:43.711
every lecture.

00:47:43.811 --> 00:47:46.614
Have a fantastic weekend
and see you Monday.

